Q: Why does a range on the first composite column stop later columns? What’s the fix?

📌 Index
(created_date, role_type, other_col)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Case 1 — Equalities only (best)

WHERE created_date = '2025-09-12'
  AND role_type = 'ADMIN';

Uses:
created_date → then role_type (tight index lookup)
Perfect.

────────────────────────────────────────
❗ Case 2 — Range on leading column

WHERE created_date > '2025-09-01'
  AND role_type = 'ADMIN';

Index leaf order looks like:
2025-09-01, ADMIN
2025-09-01, USER
2025-09-02, ADMIN
2025-09-02, USER
2025-09-03, ADMIN
...

For created_date > '2025-09-01', engine finds the start point then **scans forward** across many dates.
Within that scan, ADMIN / USER are interleaved — it cannot “jump” only to ADMIN as a separate seek.

| Column       | Typical use after range on created_date |
| ------------ | --------------------------------------- |
| created_date | ✅ index range search                    |
| role_type    | ⚠️ filter (not a further index seek)    |

Interview rule:
In a composite index, **after the first range** (`>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `BETWEEN`, sometimes `LIKE 'abc%'`),
later columns are generally **not used for further index lookup** — only for filtering
(MySQL may still apply Index Condition Pushdown on those columns, but it’s not the same as equality seek).

