??????????? Solution >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>

If the real query is mostly:

WHERE role_type = 'ADMIN'
  AND created_date > '2025-09-01'

Better index:
(role_type, created_date)

Why:
1) Equality on role_type → jump to ADMIN block
2) Range on created_date → scan only dates inside that ADMIN block

Both columns used efficiently.

Before (bad for this query): (created_date, role_type)
After  (good for this query): (role_type, created_date)

Confirm with:
EXPLAIN SELECT ...
-- want key = your index, type = range/ref, not ALL

────────────────────────────────────────
🚀 Golden rule for composite design

1) Equality columns first
2) Range column next (usually one)
3) Prefer higher selectivity earlier when it matches query patterns
4) Design index from **actual WHERE/ORDER BY**, not guesswork

Range operators to remember:
>, <, >=, <=, BETWEEN, LIKE 'prefix%'
(LIKE '%x' usually cannot use index well)

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview one-liner

“Composite indexes are left-to-right; a range on a column stops efficient use of columns after it for lookup. Put equality first, range after —
e.g. (role_type, created_date) for role = ? AND created_date > ?.”
